0,Mixtec languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixtec_language,"January 18, 2026 - One of the most characteristic features of Mixtec is its use of tones, a characteristic it shares with all other Otomanguean languages. Despite its importance in the language, the tonal analyses of Mixtec have been many and quite different one from another. Some varieties of Mixtec display ...","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
1,Atatláhuca–San Miguel Mixtec - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixtec_of_Santa_Cruz_Itundujia_language,"December 15, 2024 - Atatláhuca–San Miguel Mixtec is a diverse Mixtec language of Oaxaca.","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
2,Oto-Manguean languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oto-Manguean_languages,"April 28, 2026 - The highest number of speakers ... and the Mixtecan languages, are spoken by almost 1.5 million people combined. In central Mexico, particularly in the states of Mexico, Hidalgo, and Querétaro, the languages of the Oto-Pamean branch are spoken: the Otomi and the closely-related Mazahua have over 500,000 speakers combined. In the linguistic world of Mesoamerica, the Otomanguean family stands ...","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
3,Mixtecan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mixtecan_languages,"January 1, 2026 - The Mixtecan languages constitute a branch of the Oto-Manguean language family of Mexico. They include the Trique (or Triqui) languages, spoken by about 24,500 people; Cuicatec, spoken by about 15,000 people; and the large expanse of Mixtec languages, spoken by about 511,000 people.","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
4,"Mixtec, Atatlahuca Language (MIB) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/mib/,"Atatlahuca Mixtec is a stable indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by all in the ethnic community.","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
5,Mixtec | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/subgroup/3830/,"Eastern Otomanguean 142 · Amuzgo-Mixtecan 60 · Mixtecan 57 · Mixtec · Mixtec, Alacatlatzala mim, a language of Mexico · Mixtec, Alcozauca xta, a language of Mexico · Mixtec, Amoltepec mbz, a language of Mexico · Mixtec, Apasco-Apoala mip, a language of Mexico · Mixtec, Atatlahuca mib, a ...","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
6,Mixtecan Family | SIL Mexico,https://mexico.sil.org/language_culture/mixtec,"The most famous collection of Mixtec artifacts was found in tomb seven at the Monte Alban archaeological site in Oaxaca. Like other Otomanguean languages, the languages in the Mixtecan family are tonal, which means that the pitch with which a word is pronounced is so important that a change in the pitch can change one word into an entirely different one.","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
7,Proto Otomanguean Kinship | SIL Global,https://www.sil.org/resources/publications/entry/7897,Atatláhuca Mixtec [mib] Ocotepec Mixtec [mie] San Miguel El Grande Mixtec [mig] Peñoles Mixtec [mil] Southern Puebla Mixtec [mit] Ayutla Mixtec [miy] Coatzospan Mixtec [miz] Silacayoapan Mixtec [mks] Yosondúa Mixtec [mpm] Jamiltepec Mixtec [mxt] Metlatónoc Mixtec [mxv] Mezquital Otomi [ote] Eastern Highland Otomi [otm] Tenango Otomi [otn] Estado de México Otomi [ots] Central Pame [pbs] Chichimeca-Jonaz [pei] San Marcos Tlacoyalco Popoloca [pls] San Juan Atzingo Popoloca [poe] San Felipe Otlaltepec Popoloca [pow] Malinaltepec Me'phaa [tcf] Tlacoapa Me'phaa [tpl] Copala Triqui [trc] Chicahu,"Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
8,Mixtec.org,https://mixtec.sdsu.edu/language.html,"Mixtec belongs to the Otomanguean group of Mesoamerican languages. Proto-otomanguean, the mother language, was spoken by hunters and gatherers in the region over 10,000 years ago. A forerunner of contemporary Mixtec appeared some 7,000 years ago (Bartolomé, 1999) · It is misleading to talk ...","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
9,Mixtecan languages,https://grokipedia.com/page/mixtecan_languages,"The Mixtecan languages form a branch of the Otomanguean language stock, indigenous to Mesoamerica and primarily spoken by communities in southern Mexico across the states of Oaxaca, Puebla, and Guerrero. This family encompasses three main branches—Mixtec, Triqui (also spelled Trique), and ...","Mixtec, Atatlahuca",Otomanguean
